---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-01", modified: "2026-09-01" });
const title = "Whole-House Generator Installation Cost: What to Expect";
const description =
  "Installation often approaches or exceeds the price of the unit. Here is what drives it — gas line, electrical run, pad, transfer switch and permits.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Whole-House Generator Installation Cost",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does whole-house generator installation cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It varies widely by property, and installation frequently approaches or exceeds the price of the generator itself. The main drivers are how far the unit sits from the gas meter and electrical panel, whether the meter needs upsizing, the type of transfer switch, and the condition of your electrical service.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is standby generator installation so expensive?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because it is several trades and several permits rather than a delivery. It involves a gas connection sized for the load, a heavy electrical run to a transfer switch, panel work, a prepared pad, permits and inspection, and commissioning. Distance from the gas and electrical service drives most of the variation.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I install a whole-house generator myself?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. It involves gas connection work and altering how your home connects to the electrical grid, both of which are regulated and require licensed trades and inspection in essentially all jurisdictions. It is also the kind of work where a mistake endangers utility crews as well as your household.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the generator need to be close to the house?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It needs to meet clearance requirements from windows, doors and property lines for exhaust safety, while being as close as practical to the gas meter and electrical panel to control cost. Those two pressures sometimes conflict, and the resolution affects the installed price significantly.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What ongoing costs does a standby generator have?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Annual servicing with oil, filters and inspection, a small amount of fuel consumed by the weekly self-test, starting battery replacement every few years, and fuel during actual outages. Skipped servicing is the most common reason these units fail when called on.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a standby generator worth it compared to a portable?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on whether backup power must work unattended. If outages last days, the house is often empty, someone depends on medical equipment or a sump pump protects a finished basement, the automatic start is the product. Otherwise a large dual-fuel portable with a transfer switch covers the same circuits for far less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does whole-house generator installation take?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The installation itself is usually one to two days, but the whole project typically runs several weeks once you include the site survey, permit application, unit lead time, pad preparation, inspection and commissioning. Lead times stretch considerably after major regional outages.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Generators & Backup Power",
    tags: [
      "whole house generator installation cost",
      "standby generator install",
      "generator installation",
      "generators & backup power",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whole House Generators",
    link: "/best-whole-house-generator/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Cost Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Whole-House Generator <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Installation Cost</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The generator is the part you shop for. The installation is the part that varies by thousands depending on where your gas meter and electrical panel happen to be.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#components"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See What Drives the Cost
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#components" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What You Are Paying For</a>
          <a href="#drivers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Drives the Price</a>
          <a href="#reducing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Reducing the Cost</a>
          <a href="#ongoing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Ongoing Costs</a>
          <a href="#worth-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Is It Worth It?</a>
          <a href="#timeline" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Installation Timeline</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Two identical standby generators installed on two identical houses can differ by thousands in installed price, and the unit itself explains none of it. What explains it is distance — how far the generator sits from the gas meter and the electrical panel.
        </p>

        <div id="components" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Components of an Installed Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house generator installation is several trades and several permits, not a delivery. Understanding the line items makes quotes far easier to compare.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Component</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Involves</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost Driver</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">The generator unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The machine itself</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Capacity in kW, brand, enclosure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Switching gear and its enclosure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole-panel versus selected circuits</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete or composite pad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A level base the unit sits on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Site access and ground conditions</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas connection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Piping from meter or tank to the unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Distance, and whether the meter needs upsizing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electrical connection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cable from generator to the transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Distance and cable size</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Panel work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wiring the switch into the service</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Panel condition; may need upgrading</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Permits and inspection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrical and usually gas permits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Local requirements</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Commissioning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Start-up, testing, configuration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually included by good installers</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Installation Frequently Rivals the Unit Price</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Distance is the biggest single variable.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A generator sited a few feet from both the gas meter and the electrical panel is a straightforward job. The same generator sixty feet away, around a corner, across a driveway, needs long gas piping and heavy cable runs — and that trenching and material is where installation costs escalate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Five Things That Move the Price Most
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Distance From Gas and Electrical Service</h3>

          <p>
            The dominant factor. Gas piping has to be sized for the generator’s demand over its run length, and a long run may need larger pipe than a short one to deliver adequate pressure. The electrical cable between generator and transfer switch is similarly sized by both current and distance. Both mean trenching if the route crosses ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Whether Your Gas Meter Can Supply It</h3>

          <p>
            A frequently overlooked cost. A standby generator is a substantial gas appliance, and an existing meter sized for a furnace, water heater and range may not have the capacity to add it. Upsizing the meter is a utility job with its own lead time and cost, and it is worth asking any installer to confirm meter capacity as part of the quote.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Whole-Panel Versus Selected Circuits</h3>

          <p>
            A service-rated transfer switch backing up the entire panel costs more than one covering a selected list of circuits, and it also requires a generator sized for the whole house. Choosing selected circuits with load shedding often allows a smaller generator and a cheaper switch, and the combined saving can be substantial.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. The Condition of Your Electrical Service</h3>

          <p>
            An older panel that is full, obsolete or undersized may need upgrading before a transfer switch can be added, and a service upgrade is a significant cost in its own right. This is the item most likely to turn a predictable quote into an unwelcome surprise, so it is worth having the panel assessed early.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Site Access and Ground Conditions</h3>

          <p>
            A generator has to be craned, wheeled or carried into position, and a level pad prepared. Restricted access, sloping ground, rock, or landscaping that has to be reinstated afterwards all add labour. So does siting the unit to meet clearance requirements from windows, doors and property lines, which sometimes forces a less convenient and more expensive location.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reducing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Legitimate Ways to Reduce the Cost
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Site the generator as close as practical to both the gas meter and the electrical panel; this is the single biggest lever.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Back up selected circuits with load shedding rather than the whole panel, allowing a smaller unit and switch.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Size the generator to a realistic load list rather than to the whole house by default.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Get the panel assessed before quoting so any upgrade is priced in rather than discovered later.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Schedule outside peak season; installers are busiest immediately after major outages.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Get three quotes and compare the line items rather than the headline totals.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Sizing Conversation Is Worth Having Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Many households are quoted for whole-house capacity by default when a smaller unit covering the circuits they actually care about would serve. Central air conditioning is usually what drives the capacity requirement, and a soft starter on the condenser can reduce that surge substantially — sometimes enough to move down a generator size and save considerably more than the device costs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Compare Line Items, Not Totals</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Two quotes that look similar can include very different things.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ask specifically what transfer switch is included and whether it is whole-panel or selected circuits, whether the gas meter capacity has been verified, whether permits and inspection are in the price, whether the pad is included, and what the warranty and first-service arrangements are. Those answers explain most differences between quotes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ongoing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Costs That Continue After Installation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is the large one-off, but a standby generator is a permanently installed engine and it has running costs that are worth budgeting for from the start.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ongoing Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Frequency</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Annual service</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yearly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil, filters, plugs, inspection; often a contract</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weekly self-test fuel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weekly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small but continuous gas or propane use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fuel during outages</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">As used</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural gas is metered; propane needs refilling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery replacement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every few years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The starting battery; a common failure point</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repairs out of warranty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Occasional</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Engines that sit idle develop their own faults</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Self-Test Is a Real, If Small, Cost</h3>

          <p>
            Standby units exercise themselves on a schedule, typically weekly, to keep the engine healthy and surface faults before an outage. That consumes a modest amount of fuel every week for years. It is genuinely worth having — an unexercised generator is far more likely to fail when called on — but it is a running cost people do not anticipate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance Is Not Optional</h3>

          <p>
            The most common reason a standby generator fails during an outage is skipped servicing. An engine that runs for a few minutes a week for years, outdoors, needs its oil, filters and battery attended to. Many owners take a service contract, and if you do not, put the intervals in a calendar rather than relying on remembering.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Whether the Installed Cost Is Worth It for You
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house standby installation is one of the more expensive home improvements available, and whether it is justified depends on circumstances rather than on the technology being good — which it is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Is Usually Worth It If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Outages in your area routinely last days rather than hours.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Someone in the household depends on powered medical equipment.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A finished basement depends on a sump pump during storms.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The property is regularly empty, so a portable would never get started.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You are on a well, and outages mean no water at all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You intend to stay in the house long enough to get the value from it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Portable Plus a Transfer Switch Is Often Enough</h3>

          <p>
            For households facing occasional outages of a few hours while someone is home, a large dual-fuel portable with a manual transfer switch or interlock delivers a substantial share of the benefit at a fraction of the installed cost. It covers the same hardwired circuits — furnace, well pump, sump pump — and the one thing it cannot provide is the automatic start.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the honest dividing line. If backup power has to work when nobody is there to make it work, you are buying an automatic system and the installation cost is part of the price. If someone will reliably be home, the portable route deserves serious consideration before committing to a standby installation.
          </p>
        </div>

