          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Order Before the Season, Not During It</h3>

          <p>
            Demand for standby generators spikes immediately after every significant regional outage, and both unit availability and installer schedules stretch accordingly. Households that decide to act during a storm frequently wait months. If you know you want one, starting the process in a quiet period is the difference between weeks and a season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Permit Is Not the Bottleneck People Expect</h3>

          <p>
            Permits and inspection are often assumed to be the slow part, and sometimes they are, but unit lead times and installer availability are more commonly what determines the schedule. A good installer will tell you honestly where the delay sits, and that answer is worth asking for explicitly when comparing quotes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting for the Generator and Not the Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Installation frequently approaches or exceeds the price of the unit, driven mostly by how far the generator sits from the gas meter and electrical panel. A quote for the machine alone tells you very little about what the project will cost on your particular property.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking Gas Meter Capacity Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standby generator is a substantial gas appliance, and a meter sized for a furnace, water heater and range may not support it. Upsizing is a utility job with its own cost and lead time. Ask any installer to confirm meter capacity as part of the quote rather than discovering it mid-project.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Defaulting to Whole-House Capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many households are quoted for whole-panel backup when selected circuits with load shedding would serve, allowing a smaller generator and a cheaper transfer switch. Central air conditioning usually drives the capacity requirement, and a soft starter can sometimes reduce it by a whole generator size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Quote Totals Instead of Line Items</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two quotes at similar prices can include very different things — whole-panel versus selected-circuit switches, permits included or not, pad included or not, first service or not. Ask what is in each line, because that is where the real difference between installers shows up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Maintenance After Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common reason a standby generator fails during an outage is missed servicing. It is an engine that runs a few minutes a week outdoors for years, and it needs oil, filters and a battery attended to. Take a service contract or diary the intervals; do not rely on remembering.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does whole-house generator installation cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely by property, and installation frequently approaches or exceeds the price of the generator itself. The main drivers are how far the unit sits from the gas meter and electrical panel, whether the meter needs upsizing, the type of transfer switch, and the condition of your electrical service.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is standby generator installation so expensive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because it is several trades and several permits rather than a delivery. It involves a gas connection sized for the load, a heavy electrical run to a transfer switch, panel work, a prepared pad, permits and inspection, and commissioning. Distance from the gas and electrical service drives most of the variation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a whole-house generator myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It involves gas connection work and altering how your home connects to the electrical grid, both of which are regulated and require licensed trades and inspection in essentially all jurisdictions. It is also the kind of work where a mistake endangers utility crews as well as your household.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the generator need to be close to the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It needs to meet clearance requirements from windows, doors and property lines for exhaust safety, while being as close as practical to the gas meter and electrical panel to control cost. Those two pressures sometimes conflict, and the resolution affects the installed price significantly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What ongoing costs does a standby generator have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annual servicing with oil, filters and inspection, a small amount of fuel consumed by the weekly self-test, starting battery replacement every few years, and fuel during actual outages. Skipped servicing is the most common reason these units fail when called on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a standby generator worth it compared to a portable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether backup power must work unattended. If outages last days, the house is often empty, someone depends on medical equipment or a sump pump protects a finished basement, the automatic start is the product. Otherwise a large dual-fuel portable with a transfer switch covers the same circuits for far less.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does whole-house generator installation take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The installation itself is usually one to two days, but the whole project typically runs several weeks once you include the site survey, permit application, unit lead time, pad preparation, inspection and commissioning. Lead times stretch considerably after major regional outages.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

